      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the comment, that is a fair concern. I agree that the current split makes the TEIB implementation optional, but still leaves a TEIB-shaped service in VNET, so it is not full VNET/TEIB decoupling.\n\nA) Regarding direct dependency.\nGRE/IPIP could directly reach TEIB functionality using vlib_get_plugin_symbol, because all of them are plugins. \nHowever, IPSec is part of vnet, so it really shouldn\u0027t directly depend on a plugin. IPSec being a part of VNET and being a consumer of TEIB was the main reason why this dependency was inverted and this TEIB-specific API was introduced in VNET.\n\nB) Regarding the requirement.\nWe don\u0027t have exactly the requirement of TEIB to be independently disableable while consumers keep working, but I do not think the optionality is something this series chose also. Once TEIB is a plugin it can be excluded at build time and disabled in\nstartup.conf, so consumers have to tolerate that. Of course we could just hard-fail on init stage if TEIB is not present, but that would not simplify architecture in my opinion.\n\n---\n\nGiven that VNET cannot depend on a plugin, I see 3 directions:\n\n1) Accept current idea.\nVnet boundary for TEIB is fine as the only public interface for the TEIB. This way, TEIB as a plugin can be disabled, and consumers (plugins OR vnet components) can conditionally depend on TEIB, deciding what to do if it\u0027s not available.\n\n2) Redesign the VNET-owned TEIB part.\nMove listener registry and public API to TEIB plugin. Plugin consumers use that API directly (through vlib_get_plugin_symbol). Availability check is simplified to a state where we just check if the plugin symbol can be loaded.\nVNET-owned TEIB part is redesigned in a way that it contains minimal extension point for the consumers from VNET to be able to communicate with TEIB. This way it is not a main public API anymore and basically, it\u0027s temporary structure inside VNET that can be removed once all TEIB consumers are moved to plugins. \nI\u0027m actually not 100% sure what you mean by \u0027consumer-owned extension boundary\u0027, because currently it\u0027s not really consumer-owned. We can make it consumer-owned, so IPSec declares some functions that are being called by TEIB plugin, but I\u0027m not sure if that what you meant. If I misunderstand anything in your comment, please, let me know. \n\n3) Postpone moving TEIB into a pluigin. \nReject this and mark VPP-2056 as \u0027blocked by VPP-2047 (Convert IPSEC to a plugin)\u0027 and keep TEIB in VNET until IPsec is moved to a plugin. Direct dependency is simpler and this is the only way it can be done to keep it. Once IPsec is a plugin, we will have no VNET-consumers and the story is different. \n\n---\n\nWhat do you think?",
